 dict(pmid='43046111', doi='10.1186/s13690-026-02031-w', journal='Arch Public Health', title='Assessing the role of ventilation and indoor air quality in SARS-CoV-2 transmission risk in Belgian nursing homes: the AIR-CO study.', short='AIR-CO Flanders 11 nursing homes 55 rooms CO2 PM2.5 RH ventilation type SARS-CoV-2 air and surface RNA', design='Cross-sectional environmental survey', pm='One-week continuous indoor PM2.5, CO2, temperature and RH per room; instrument not stated in abstract', geo='Belgium (Flanders)', endpoint='IAQ by ventilation type; environmental SARS-CoV-2 RNA detection', tier='C', affil='Sciensano, Belgium; Sciensano, Belgium', n='Pilot in 11 Flemish nursing homes, January-May 2022: five rooms each (55 rooms) monitored for one week for CO2, PM2.5, temperature and RH, with one air sample and three surface swabs per room for SARS-CoV-2 RNA. Nine homes had full mechanical ventilation. CO2 mostly stayed within limits; RH was consistently below recommended ranges and temperatures often too high. PM2.5 was comparable across ventilation types, higher in some naturally ventilated spaces; no PM values are given. 61.9% of swabs and 27.5% of air samples were positive, with no consistent link to IAQ parameters. One natural-ventilation home and one week per room give no power for the comparison. Relevance: moderate - continuous PM/CO2 monitoring of care homes is a natural low-cost sensor application.', sub=corpus.OCC),
 dict(pmid='', doi='10.1016/j.atmosenv.2026.122502', journal='Atmospheric Environment', title='Long-term exposure to air pollution variability and incidence of cardiovascular diseases: Evidence from the China Health and Retirement Longitudinal Study (CHARLS)', short='CHARLS long-term air pollution variability incident CVD (metadata only)', design='Metadata only', pm='Metadata only - long-term variability of air pollution exposure in CHARLS', geo='China', endpoint='Metadata only - incident cardiovascular disease', tier='C', affil='not stated in record', n='Metadata only: no abstract was retrievable from PubMed, Europe PMC or Crossref at issue time. The record (Atmospheric Environment; Ni, Wu, Huang, Ye and colleagues) examines long-term variability in air-pollution exposure, rather than mean level, and incident cardiovascular disease in the China Health and Retirement Longitudinal Study. No finding is asserted here. Relevance: moderate in principle - exposure variability is a quantity that temporally dense sensor data estimate far better than annual model surfaces.', sub=corpus.CVM),
 dict(pmid='', doi='10.1021/acs.est.6c10159', journal='Environ Sci Technol', title='The Acute Exposure Window: A Missing Dimension in Industrial Fire Assessment', short='CNR Italy industrial fire acute exposure window perspective (metadata only)', design='Metadata only', pm='Metadata only - acute smoke exposure during industrial fires', geo='Italy', endpoint='Metadata only - exposure assessment framing for industrial fires', tier='C', affil='Institute of Clinical Physiology, CNR, Italy; Institute of Bioeconomy, CNR, Italy', n='Metadata only: no abstract was retrievable at issue time. The record (Environmental Science & Technology; Linzalone, Vivani, Zaldei; CNR Institutes of Clinical Physiology and Bioeconomy, Italy) argues that the acute exposure window is missing from industrial fire assessment. No finding is asserted here. Relevance: potentially direct - the last author group has worked on low-cost sensor platforms, and acute fire plumes are the case where rapidly deployed sensors would capture the hours-scale peak that regulatory networks miss.', sub=corpus.BURD),
